WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.408
Quoting evidence first.

2
00:00:01.688 --> 00:00:09.624
A related technique works with or without the citations feature: ask the model to pull out the relevant quotes before it answers.

3
00:00:09.904 --> 00:00:14.576
Anthropic’s long-context guidance recommends this for long-document tasks.

4
00:00:14.856 --> 00:00:21.448
Extracting quotes into their own tags first helps the model focus on the relevant parts and ignore the rest.

5
00:00:21.728 --> 00:00:25.184
Then the answer is written from the quotes, not from the whole pile.

6
00:00:25.464 --> 00:00:28.728
Quote-first has two engineering benefits beyond quality.

7
00:00:29.008 --> 00:00:37.392
The quotes are an intermediate output you can check before you spend tokens on an answer: if none of them verify, you can stop and abstain.

8
00:00:37.672 --> 00:00:50.835
And when you review failures later, the quotes show you what the model thought was relevant, which is how you tell a retrieval problem (the right passage wasn’t there) from a synthesis problem (it was there, and the model ignored it).

9
00:00:51.115 --> 00:00:57.451
The cost is latency and tokens, so measure whether it helps on your eval set before making it the default.

10
00:00:57.731 --> 00:01:05.880
On short, single-passage questions it often won’t; on questions that need two or three passages combined, it is more likely to.
